1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,384
This is MATH 239, video 4. The topic is combinatorial proofs: we count one set in two ways.

2
00:00:08,834 --> 00:00:14,039
After this video, you can prove an identity by counting one set in two ways.

3
00:00:14,489 --> 00:00:20,697
You can read a sum as a split into disjoint cases, and a product as a sequence of choices.

4
00:00:21,147 --> 00:00:24,475
You can prove the Vandermonde convolution formula.

5
00:00:24,925 --> 00:00:36,083
And you can solve tutorial questions T1-2 and T1-3 b, assignment question A1-2, and Practice Midterm question 1.

6
00:00:36,533 --> 00:00:51,317
In video 3, we proved that two sets have the same size with a bijection. A second method uses only one set. We count it in two different ways. Both counts give the size of the same set, so they are equal.

7
00:00:51,767 --> 00:00:55,329
Here is the set S of all subsets of 1, 2, 3.

8
00:00:55,779 --> 00:01:03,054
Count 1: sort the subsets by size. Each subset has exactly one size, so the groups are disjoint.

9
00:01:03,504 --> 00:01:11,632
Disjoint cases, joined with OR, give a sum. Column k has 3 choose k subsets: 1, 3, 3 and 1.

10
00:01:12,082 --> 00:01:16,519
So the total is 1 plus 3 plus 3 plus 1, which is 8.

11
00:01:16,969 --> 00:01:21,129
Count 2: decide for each element whether it is in or out.

12
00:01:21,579 --> 00:01:29,750
These are 3 choices, joined with AND. A sequence of choices gives a product: 2 times 2 times 2, which is 8.

13
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:37,667
Both counts give the size of the same set. So 1 plus 3 plus 3 plus 1 equals 2 to the 3.

14
00:01:38,117 --> 00:01:45,839
In general, the sum over k of n choose k equals 2 to the n. This is Example 1.6 of the notes.

15
00:01:46,289 --> 00:01:49,724
The notes call this method: think of what the numbers mean.

16
00:01:50,174 --> 00:02:01,929
Here is a set that we can count in two orders. Its elements are pairs S, a. S is a 3 element subset of 1 to 5, and a is one marked element of S.

17
00:02:02,379 --> 00:02:13,771
Each row is one subset S. Each blue cell is one pair. For example, the first row is the subset 1, 2, 3, with the pairs a equals 1, 2 and 3.

18
00:02:14,221 --> 00:02:19,149
Count 1: choose S first. There are 5 choose 3, which is 10, rows.

19
00:02:19,599 --> 00:02:26,575
Each row has 3 cells, one for each choice of a. So there are 10 times 3, which is 30, pairs.

20
00:02:27,025 --> 00:02:33,510
Count 2: choose a first. Look at the cells with a equal to 1, in orange. There are 6 of them.

21
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:43,048
Each one is a subset that contains 1. Its other 2 elements come from 2, 3, 4, 5. That is 4 choose 2, which is 6.

22
00:02:43,498 --> 00:02:50,303
The same holds for each of the 5 values of a. So there are 5 times 6, which is 30, pairs.

23
00:02:50,753 --> 00:02:58,284
Both counts give the size of the same set. So 3 times 5 choose 3 equals 5 times 4 choose 2.

24
00:02:58,734 --> 00:03:10,275
Tutorial question T1-2. By counting one set in two ways, prove that 7 times n choose 7 equals n times n minus 1 choose 6.

25
00:03:10,725 --> 00:03:12,368
Pause the video and try it.

26
00:03:12,818 --> 00:03:24,658
The 7 and the n suggest a marked element. Let P be the set of pairs S, a. S is a 7 element subset of an n element set, and a is an element of S.

27
00:03:25,108 --> 00:03:28,457
Count 1: choose S first, in n choose 7 ways.

28
00:03:28,907 --> 00:03:35,073
Then choose a in S, in 7 ways. So P has 7 times n choose 7 elements.

29
00:03:35,523 --> 00:03:37,933
Count 2: choose a first, in n ways.

30
00:03:38,383 --> 00:03:48,111
Then choose the other 6 elements of S from the remaining n minus 1 elements. So P has n times n minus 1 choose 6 elements.

31
00:03:48,561 --> 00:03:54,236
Both expressions count the same set P, so they are equal. This is the full solution.

32
00:03:54,686 --> 00:04:09,086
Check with n equals 9. 9 choose 7 is 36, and 7 times 36 is 252. 8 choose 6 is 28, and 9 times 28 is 252.

33
00:04:09,536 --> 00:04:18,923
The same proof, with k in place of 7, shows that k times n choose k equals n times n minus 1 choose k minus 1.

34
00:04:19,373 --> 00:04:28,973
Notes Exercise 1.7 a uses the same kind of set, but S can have any size. The set is all pairs S, a, with a in S.

35
00:04:29,423 --> 00:04:36,932
Here n is 3. Each row of cells is one subset S. Each cell is one pair S, a, and it shows a.

36
00:04:37,382 --> 00:04:42,865
Count 1: split by the size k of S. The cases are disjoint, so we add.

37
00:04:43,315 --> 00:04:54,621
Size k gives n choose k subsets, with k cells each. For n equals 3: 0 times 1, 1 times 3, 2 times 3, and 3 times 1.

38
00:04:55,071 --> 00:05:03,583
So count 1 is the sum over k of k times n choose k. Here 0 plus 3 plus 6 plus 3 is 12.

39
00:05:04,033 --> 00:05:11,073
Count 2: choose a first, in n ways. The cells with a equal to 1 are orange. There are 4 of them.

40
00:05:11,523 --> 00:05:22,318
The rest of S is any subset of the other n minus 1 elements, here 2 and 3. That gives 2 to the n minus 1 choices. Here 2 squared is 4.

41
00:05:22,768 --> 00:05:32,283
The same holds for a equal to 2 and to 3. So count 2 is n times 2 to the n minus 1. Here 3 times 4 is 12.

42
00:05:32,733 --> 00:05:39,111
So the sum over k of k times n choose k equals n times 2 to the n minus 1.

43
00:05:39,561 --> 00:05:47,476
The notes ask for a bijective proof. This double count is the same proof: both sides count one set of pairs.

44
00:05:47,926 --> 00:06:01,046
Example 1.19 is the Vandermonde convolution formula. m plus n choose k equals the sum over j of m choose j times n choose k minus j.

45
00:06:01,496 --> 00:06:14,403
Take m equals 4, n equals 2, and k equals 3. The left side is 6 choose 3. That is 6 times 5 times 4, over 3 times 2 times 1, which is 20.

46
00:06:14,853 --> 00:06:19,717
Split 1 to 6 into M, the first 4 numbers, and N, the last 2.

47
00:06:20,167 --> 00:06:24,689
Sort the 20 subsets by j, the number of their elements in M.

48
00:06:25,139 --> 00:06:34,355
j equals 1: one element from M in 4 ways, and both elements of N in 1 way. 4 times 1 is 4 subsets.

49
00:06:34,805 --> 00:06:43,339
j equals 2: two elements from M in 6 ways, and one from N in 2 ways. 6 times 2 is 12 subsets.

50
00:06:43,789 --> 00:06:51,981
j equals 3: all three from M in 4 ways, and none from N in 1 way. 4 times 1 is 4 subsets.

51
00:06:52,431 --> 00:06:59,407
j equals 0 would need 3 elements from N, which has only 2. So 2 choose 3 is 0.

52
00:06:59,857 --> 00:07:06,065
The total is 0 plus 4 plus 12 plus 4, which is 20. The two sides agree.

53
00:07:06,515 --> 00:07:13,768
Here is the proof in the notes. The left side counts the k element subsets S of 1 to m plus n.

54
00:07:14,218 --> 00:07:22,943
The right side counts a choice of j, AND a j element subset A of M, AND a k minus j element subset B of N.

55
00:07:23,393 --> 00:07:28,044
Here m is 4, n is 2, k is 3, and S is 2, 4, 5.

56
00:07:28,494 --> 00:07:33,870
The map cuts S into two parts. A is S intersect M, which is 2, 4.

57
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:37,221
B is S intersect N, which is 5.

58
00:07:37,671 --> 00:07:43,687
So j is 2, and B has k minus j elements: 3 minus 2, which is 1.

59
00:07:44,137 --> 00:07:49,897
The inverse map joins the parts. A union B is 2, 4, 5, which is S again.

60
00:07:50,347 --> 00:07:58,966
M and N have no common element, and together they are all of 1 to m plus n. So cutting and joining undo each other.

61
00:07:59,416 --> 00:08:06,883
By Proposition 1.11, both sides count the same number of objects. This proves the formula.

62
00:08:07,333 --> 00:08:20,367
Assignment question A1-2. By counting one set in two ways, prove that 2 n choose n equals the sum over k of n choose k times n choose n minus k.

63
00:08:20,817 --> 00:08:22,460
Pause the video and try it.

64
00:08:22,910 --> 00:08:33,662
The left side suggests the set S of all n element subsets of 1 to 2 n. Count 1 is the definition: S has 2 n choose n elements.

65
00:08:34,112 --> 00:08:41,835
The picture uses n equals 3. So the first half is 1, 2, 3, and the second half is 4, 5, 6.

66
00:08:42,285 --> 00:08:51,885
Count 2: split S by k, the number of elements in the first half. Let S k be the subsets with exactly k elements in 1 to n.

67
00:08:52,335 --> 00:09:05,689
To build a member of S k, choose k elements from the first half, AND n minus k elements from the second half. So S k has n choose k times n choose n minus k elements.

68
00:09:06,139 --> 00:09:14,097
k equals 0: all 3 elements come from the second half. 1 times 1 is 1 subset. The row shows it.

69
00:09:14,547 --> 00:09:20,349
k equals 1: 3 times 3, which is 9, subsets. The row shows one of them.

70
00:09:20,799 --> 00:09:24,447
k equals 2: 3 times 3 again, which is 9.

71
00:09:24,897 --> 00:09:28,183
k equals 3: 1 times 1, which is 1.

72
00:09:28,633 --> 00:09:40,921
Each subset is in exactly one S k. So S is a disjoint union, and its size is the sum. 1 plus 9 plus 9 plus 1 is 20, which is 6 choose 3.

73
00:09:41,371 --> 00:09:45,765
Here is the written solution. First, name the set, and give count 1.

74
00:09:46,215 --> 00:10:00,573
Second, define the parts S k. Say why the parts are disjoint and cover S: each subset has exactly one number of elements in the first half. So the size of S is the sum of the sizes of the parts.

75
00:10:01,023 --> 00:10:07,999
Third, count one part. Choose k elements from the first half, and n minus k from the second half.

76
00:10:08,449 --> 00:10:17,921
Fourth, put the two counts together. This is the identity. It is the special case m equals n equals k of the Vandermonde formula.

77
00:10:18,371 --> 00:10:28,952
The bonus parts of A1-2 are short. Part a: the identity is the same as 2 n choose n equals the sum of the squares of n choose k.

78
00:10:29,402 --> 00:10:37,573
Here n is 3 and k is 1. We choose n minus k, which is 2, elements of the second half: here 4 and 6.

79
00:10:38,023 --> 00:10:44,935
Choosing the 2 elements that are in is the same as choosing the 1 element that is out: here 5.

80
00:10:45,385 --> 00:10:51,849
The map, take the complement, is a bijection. So n choose n minus k equals n choose k.

81
00:10:52,299 --> 00:11:07,893
So each product n choose k times n choose n minus k is a square. For n equals 3: 1 squared plus 3 squared plus 3 squared plus 1 squared is 1 plus 9 plus 9 plus 1, which is 20.

82
00:11:08,343 --> 00:11:13,954
Part b: cut 1 to n after position l, not after position n over 2.

83
00:11:14,404 --> 00:11:27,012
Let i be the number of elements in the first part. The same argument gives n choose k equals the sum over i of l choose i times n minus l choose k minus i.

84
00:11:27,462 --> 00:11:31,195
This is the Vandermonde formula with m equal to l.

85
00:11:31,645 --> 00:11:43,997
Tutorial question T1-3 has two parts. Part a: explain why the number of k element multisets from 1 to n plus 1 is n plus k choose k.

86
00:11:44,447 --> 00:11:51,103
Here is an example with n equals 2 and k equals 3. The multiset is 1, 3, 3.

87
00:11:51,553 --> 00:11:58,743
Write a star for each element, and a bar between neighbouring types. Type 1 has one star. Then a bar.

88
00:11:59,193 --> 00:12:02,073
Type 2 has no star. Then a bar.

89
00:12:02,523 --> 00:12:04,443
Type 3 has two stars.

90
00:12:04,893 --> 00:12:13,639
The stars between the bars give the multiplicity vector: m 1 equals 1, m 2 equals 0, m 3 equals 2.

91
00:12:14,089 --> 00:12:19,999
There are k equals 3 stars and n equals 2 bars. So there are 5 positions.

92
00:12:20,449 --> 00:12:36,001
A multiset is the same as a choice of the k star positions. So there are n plus k choose k multisets. Here 5 choose 3 is 5 times 4 times 3, over 3 times 2 times 1, which is 10.

93
00:12:36,451 --> 00:12:40,675
This is Theorem 1.9 with t equals n plus 1 types.

94
00:12:41,125 --> 00:12:50,874
Part b: count the same multisets in a second way. Split them by j, the number of elements from 1 to n, counted with multiplicity.

95
00:12:51,324 --> 00:12:52,967
Pause the video and try it.

96
00:12:53,417 --> 00:13:06,259
Fix j. The j elements from 1 to n form a j element multiset with n types. By Theorem 1.9, there are n plus j minus 1 choose j of them.

97
00:13:06,709 --> 00:13:17,525
The other k minus j elements must all be copies of n plus 1. There is only one way to do that. In the picture, the copies of 3 are purple.

98
00:13:17,975 --> 00:13:24,845
Here n is 2. j equals 0: 1 choose 0, which is 1, multiset: 3, 3, 3.

99
00:13:25,295 --> 00:13:29,220
j equals 1: 2 choose 1, which is 2, multisets.

100
00:13:29,670 --> 00:13:33,809
j equals 2: 3 choose 2, which is 3, multisets.

101
00:13:34,259 --> 00:13:41,384
j equals 3: 4 choose 3, which is 4, multisets. None of them uses the element 3.

102
00:13:41,834 --> 00:13:49,599
Every multiset has exactly one value of j, from 0 to k. So the total is the sum over j.

103
00:13:50,049 --> 00:13:58,327
Here 1 plus 2 plus 3 plus 4 equals 10, which is 5 choose 3. With part a, this proves the identity.

104
00:13:58,777 --> 00:14:08,505
Practice Midterm question 1, worth 10 marks. S is the set of pairs A, B, where A and B are k element subsets of 1 to n.

105
00:14:08,955 --> 00:14:11,749
Count S in two ways to prove the identity.

106
00:14:12,199 --> 00:14:17,639
The hint says: look at the size of A intersect B. Pause the video and try it.

107
00:14:18,089 --> 00:14:26,260
Count 1 is direct. Choose A in n choose k ways, and then B in n choose k ways. B does not depend on A.

108
00:14:26,710 --> 00:14:31,297
So S has n choose k squared elements. This is the left side.

109
00:14:31,747 --> 00:14:40,643
Count 2 follows the hint. Each pair has one value of i, the size of A intersect B. i is between 0 and k.

110
00:14:41,093 --> 00:14:48,367
So S is a disjoint union of the sets of pairs with a given i, and its size is a sum over i.

111
00:14:48,817 --> 00:14:51,697
In the picture, A and B share i elements.

112
00:14:52,147 --> 00:14:58,953
So A has k minus i elements outside B, and B has k minus i elements outside A.

113
00:14:59,403 --> 00:15:07,680
Build a pair with a given i in three steps. The example has n equals 5, k equals 2 and i equals 1.

114
00:15:08,130 --> 00:15:15,213
First, choose the common part, A intersect B. That is i elements from n, in n choose i ways.

115
00:15:15,663 --> 00:15:20,356
Here we choose 3. That is one of 5 choose 1, which is 5, ways.

116
00:15:20,806 --> 00:15:35,974
Second, choose the rest of A, that is A minus B. It has k minus i elements. It cannot use the common part, so it comes from the other n minus i elements, in n minus i choose k minus i ways.

117
00:15:36,424 --> 00:15:44,360
Here 3 is used, so 4 elements are left. We choose 1. That is one of 4 choose 1, which is 4, ways.

118
00:15:44,810 --> 00:16:01,407
Third, choose the rest of B, that is B minus A. It has k minus i elements, and it must avoid all of A. A has k elements, so n minus k elements are left. That gives n minus k choose k minus i ways.

119
00:16:01,857 --> 00:16:09,644
Here A is 1, 3, so 3 elements are left. We choose 4. That is one of 3 choose 1, which is 3, ways.

120
00:16:10,094 --> 00:16:18,542
So A is 1, 3, and B is 3, 4. For i equals 1, there are 5 times 4 times 3, which is 60, pairs.

121
00:16:18,992 --> 00:16:26,139
Each pair is built exactly once: A and B give back the three parts, and the three parts give back A and B.

122
00:16:26,589 --> 00:16:32,029
So the number of pairs with this i is the product of the three binomial coefficients.

123
00:16:32,479 --> 00:16:41,652
Check the formula with n equals 5 and k equals 2. For i equals 0: 1 times 10 times 3, which is 30.

124
00:16:42,102 --> 00:16:46,753
For i equals 1: 5 times 4 times 3, which is 60.

125
00:16:47,203 --> 00:16:51,469
For i equals 2: 10 times 1 times 1, which is 10.

126
00:16:51,919 --> 00:17:04,079
The sum is 30 plus 60 plus 10, which is 100. That is 10 squared, and 10 is 5 choose 2. A computer check lists all pairs for n up to 8 and agrees.

127
00:17:04,529 --> 00:17:08,220
Now the written solution. Count 1: choose A, then B.

128
00:17:08,670 --> 00:17:19,998
Count 2: define S i as the pairs with i common elements. The S i are disjoint and cover S, so the size of S is the sum of their sizes.

129
00:17:20,448 --> 00:17:26,251
Count one S i with the three choices. Say why each pair arises exactly once.

130
00:17:26,701 --> 00:17:32,909
The two counts give the size of the same set S. So they are equal. This is the identity.

131
00:17:33,359 --> 00:17:41,593
Here is the form of a combinatorial proof. Part 1: define one set S. Describe its objects in words.

132
00:17:42,043 --> 00:17:45,329
Part 2: count S in a first way, and get one side.

133
00:17:45,779 --> 00:17:53,587
Usually the simpler side tells you the set. 2 n choose n gives the n element subsets of 1 to 2 n.

134
00:17:54,037 --> 00:17:57,685
A factor like 7 or n suggests a marked element.

135
00:17:58,135 --> 00:18:01,761
Part 3: count S in a second way, and get the other side.

136
00:18:02,211 --> 00:18:07,993
A sum means disjoint cases. Say why every object is in exactly one case.

137
00:18:08,443 --> 00:18:11,088
A product means a sequence of choices.

138
00:18:11,538 --> 00:18:16,957
Part 4: conclude that the two sides are equal, because they count the same set.

139
00:18:17,407 --> 00:18:23,807
To recap. A combinatorial proof counts one set in two ways, and the two counts are equal.

140
00:18:24,257 --> 00:18:29,355
A sum means disjoint cases. A product means a sequence of choices.

141
00:18:29,805 --> 00:18:32,813
We used a marked element for T1-2.

142
00:18:33,263 --> 00:18:36,997
We split a set into two parts for the Vandermonde formula.

143
00:18:37,447 --> 00:18:43,676
We split by how many elements come from one part for A1-2 and T1-3 b.

144
00:18:44,126 --> 00:18:50,910
And we split by the size of the overlap for Practice Midterm question 1, and then made three choices.

145
00:18:51,360 --> 00:18:56,160
In the next video, we count sets with overlaps: inclusion and exclusion.
